        "at": "Step 1: Understanding the Client",
        "say": "Alright, this is Financial Planning — the seven-step process that is the spine of the whole exam, so let's build it one step at a time. Step one is understanding the client's personal and financial circumstances, and that means gathering two kinds of data. Quantitative data is the objective, measurable stuff — assets, liabilities, income, expenses, tax returns, insurance policies, investment statements, wills, trusts, and account balances. Qualitative data is the subjective stuff — goals, values, health, risk tolerance, attitudes, and family circumstances. Here's the tell they test constantly: if you can put a number on it, it's quantitative; if it's a feeling or a goal, it's qualitative. So the classic which is NOT quantitative answer is always the client's personal goals. Now the incomplete information rule, because this is a favorite trap. If you can't get sufficient and relevant quantitative information, you have exactly two choices — restrict the scope of the engagement to what you do have good data for, or terminate the engagement. You may never proceed on inadequate data. One more small detail they slip in: approved documentation methods are CRM software, handwritten notes, and emails — voice memos are not approved. And note, determining a client's strengths and weaknesses, which comes in step three, is a subjective process even though it sits inside all this data gathering."
