        "say": "Where the balance sheet is a snapshot, the cash flow statement is a movie — it shows money moving over a period. The formula is simple: Net Cash Flow equals Total Income minus Total Outflows. What the exam tests is the ORDER of those outflows, because there is a deliberate philosophy baked into it. Savings and investments are listed first — that is the pay yourself first principle, and it is not an accident that it sits at the top. After savings come your fixed outflows — rent or mortgage, insurance premiums, loan payments — the bills that don't move. Then variable outflows, the ones you can flex — food, clothing, utilities, entertainment. And taxes come last on the list — income taxes plus FICA and payroll taxes. Now circle back to that credit card point, because it links the two statements. When you swipe the card, that is NOT a cash outflow yet — it becomes a liability on the balance sheet. The cash outflow only happens later, when you actually pay the bill. Mix those up and your net cash flow will be wrong. Two more numbers to bank: the savings ratio target is ten to fifteen percent of gross income directed to savings and investments. And the vocabulary — a surplus is a positive net cash flow, income beating outflows, while a deficit is negative, outflows beating income."
