        "say": "Emergency funds first, and the trigger is how stable the household income is. Three months of expenses is enough when both spouses are employed, or a single person has significant secondary income — the idea being there's a backup earner if one paycheck stops. But six months is the target for a single wage earner, or a married couple living on only one income, because there is no cushion behind that one stream. Now, WHERE you park an emergency fund matters just as much as how big it is — it has to be liquid and safe. Appropriate homes are checking, savings, money market deposit accounts, money market mutual funds, and CDs maturing in ninety days or less. What is NOT appropriate — and this is a favorite distractor — is stocks, bonds, or IRAs, because they're either too volatile or too illiquid when you need cash tomorrow. Lines of credit are a backup only, not the fund itself. On budgeting, know three techniques by name. Zero-based budgeting assigns every single dollar of income a job, so total budget equals total income — it forces deliberate choices. The envelope method gives each spending category its own envelope, physical or digital, and when the envelope is empty, the spending stops. And pay yourself first automatically pulls savings out before any discretionary spending gets allocated — the text calls it the most effective savings strategy, which is exactly why it topped the cash-flow outflow order."
