        "say": "Alright, this is Education Planning — and the centerpiece is the three-step funding calculation, which the exam tests almost every sitting, so let's walk it slowly. The whole idea is to figure out one number: how much a client needs today, or each year, to pay for a future college bill. Step one, Inflate: in END mode, at the education inflation rate, you grow the current annual cost up to the first-year college cost. Step two, Adjust: and here's the key, you switch to BEG mode, because tuition is due at the beginning of each year, so it's an annuity due. You discount all the college years back using the inflation-adjusted rate, and that gives you the lump sum needed on day one of college. That special rate is one plus the return, divided by one plus education inflation, minus one. Step three, Invest: back in END mode at the investment return, you solve for the present value needed today, or the level annual payment. Take Mary, age three, college in fifteen years, tuition ten thousand dollars today, education inflation six percent, return eight percent. Inflating gives a first-year cost near twenty-four thousand dollars; adjusting gives a lump sum of about ninety-three thousand two hundred dollars on day one; and investing back means roughly twenty-nine thousand four hundred dollars today, or about thirty-four hundred dollars a year level. One more choice: serial payments start lower but grow with inflation, so you prefer them when income is rising; level payments win on simplicity or flat income."
