        "at": "Types of Life Insurance",
        "say": "Alright, this is Life Insurance — the heart of the course, and the exam leans on it hard, so let's get the product ladder straight first. Term life is pure protection — no cash value, cheap, and convertible to permanent coverage without new evidence of insurability. Whole life is the opposite end: level premiums for life, guaranteed cash value, a permanent death benefit, and it can be participating, meaning it pays dividends. In the middle sit the flexible-premium products. Universal life unbundles the policy — flexible premiums, an adjustable death benefit, and interest credited at a current rate that is not guaranteed. Then the variable products, and here is the pair they love. Variable life has fixed premiums and a guaranteed minimum death benefit — part of your premium funds that floor, part goes to subaccounts. Variable universal life has flexible premiums and NO guaranteed death benefit — everything goes to the subaccounts, so if those subaccounts fall to zero, the policy lapses. Variable life's guaranteed benefit survives; V-U-L's does not. Both variable products are registered securities. Now the universal life death-benefit options, tested through the net amount at risk — the pure-insurance slice, death benefit minus cash value, because that is what the mortality charge hits. Option A keeps the benefit level, so as cash value grows the net amount at risk shrinks — cheaper. Option B pays face plus cash value, so the net amount at risk stays level — costlier. The trap: under Option A the cash value is absorbed into the face, not paid on top. On a three hundred thousand dollar policy with seventy-five thousand of cash value, Option A pays three hundred thousand; Option B pays three hundred seventy-five thousand."
