        "say": "This is the highest-yield tax content in the course, so let's nail the traps. Start with the good news: death benefits are generally income-tax-free under Section 101, and cash value grows tax-deferred inside the policy. For living access to a normal, non-MEC policy, withdrawals are F-I-F-O — first-in, first-out — so your basis comes out first tax-free, and only gains above it are taxable. Policy loans are not taxable while the policy stays in force, but here is the trap: if the policy lapses with a loan outstanding, the excess over basis becomes immediately taxable — a nasty surprise. Now the modified endowment contract. A policy becomes a MEC if it fails the seven-pay test, meaning it was funded too fast; and once a MEC, always a MEC — the status even follows it through a 1035 exchange. When a MEC gives you living access the rules flip: withdrawals are L-I-F-O, gains out first as ordinary income, plus a ten percent penalty before age fifty-nine and a half, and even loans are taxable. A MEC can arise in any cash-value policy but never in term, which has no cash value to over-fund. The point they test: the death benefit is income-tax-free either way — MEC status only bites on living access. On surrender, gain equals cash received minus basis, where basis is premiums paid minus prior dividends and prior tax-free withdrawals — that is why Susan's fifteen thousand dollar basis against a twenty-eight thousand surrender is thirteen thousand of ordinary income, never capital gain. Two more: the transfer-for-value rule can make death proceeds taxable if a policy is sold, unless it lands with the insured, a partner, or certain business entities. And a Section 1035 exchange is a tax-free swap — life into life, endowment, annuity, or qualified long-term care — but never backwards into life insurance. Finally, group term: the first fifty thousand dollars of employer-paid coverage is tax-free; above that, Table One imputed income shows up on the W-2."
