        "say": "This is the alphabet soup, and the exam loves to make you untangle it, so slow down. All of these are riders that guarantee something extra. The G-L-W-B, the guaranteed lifetime withdrawal benefit, lets you withdraw a stated percentage of a benefit base every year for life, even if the account value falls to zero — it is the most popular rider. The G-M-I-B, guaranteed minimum income benefit, gives you the right to annuitize at a guaranteed rate no matter what the account is worth — an income floor. The G-M-W-B, guaranteed minimum withdrawal benefit, lets you withdraw until your total withdrawals equal your premium — that is principal recovery. The G-M-A-B guarantees the account value equals at least your premium after a set period, and the G-M-D-B guarantees the death benefit is at least your premium or a stepped-up amount. These riders cost roughly half a percent to one and a half percent a year on top of the mortality and expense charges, so always weigh cost against value. The machinery to understand is that every rider runs on two separate numbers. The account value is the real money — what you can surrender or leave to heirs. The benefit base is a bookkeeping figure used only to calculate the guarantee; you cannot withdraw it as a lump sum, and it grows by the greater of a roll-up rate, say five percent a year, or a step-up that locks in market highs. That is how a G-L-W-B keeps paying five percent of a two hundred thousand dollar benefit base for life even after the real account hits zero. The landmine: excess withdrawals above the guaranteed amount usually cut the benefit base proportionally and permanently. And the distinction they test — G-M-I-B has the word income and forces you to annuitize; G-M-W-B says withdrawal but ends once you have pulled out your premium; G-L-W-B is the L for Lifetime, income continues for life without annuitizing. If a client wants lifetime income but will not give up access to the money, the answer is G-L-W-B."
