        "say": "This is where students lose easy points confusing the plan types, so let's sort them cleanly. Six show up: indemnity or fee-for-service, HMO, PPO, POS, EPO, and the high-deductible plan paired with an HSA. Two terms first — capitation means the primary-care doctor is paid a fixed monthly fee per enrolled patient no matter how much care they give, and a gatekeeper is a primary-care physician who controls all specialist referrals. Now the trick to keep the four managed plans straight is to ask just two questions: is there a gatekeeper, and is there any out-of-network coverage? The HMO says gatekeeper yes, out-of-network no — cheapest and most restrictive. The PPO says gatekeeper no, out-of-network yes — most flexible and priciest. The POS is the hybrid — gatekeeper yes like an HMO, but some out-of-network benefit like a PPO. And the EPO says no referrals but no out-of-network either, emergencies only. The tell: only the HMO and POS use a gatekeeper, and only the PPO and POS pay anything out-of-network. On the math, remember the formula — expense minus deductible, times the insurer's percentage, equals what the insurer pays. Take Frank's twenty-five thousand dollar hospital stay with a one thousand dollar deductible, twenty percent coinsurance, and a five thousand dollar max out-of-pocket. He pays the deductible, then twenty percent of the remaining twenty-four thousand would be forty-eight hundred, but the max out-of-pocket caps his total at five thousand dollars — so the MOOP, not the coinsurance, is his ceiling. And unreimbursed medical expenses are deductible only above seven and a half percent of AGI."
