        "say": "Alright, this is Disability and LTC — protecting the paycheck and the nursing-home bill, the two risks that quietly wreck a financial plan. Start with why it matters: a healthy thirty-five-year-old has roughly a twenty-one to twenty-four percent chance of being disabled for three months or more before retirement, and disability income insurance replaces only about two thirds of your pay, that is sixty to seventy percent, never the whole thing. Now the single most tested idea here is the definition of disability, ranked easiest to hardest to collect on. Own-occupation is the broadest and priciest — you are disabled if you cannot perform your specific job, even if you go earn a living in another field, and that is the one professionals want. Modified own-occ, sometimes called split definition, uses own-occ for a first period, then converts to any-occ. Any-occupation is the narrowest private definition: you are disabled only if you cannot do any job you are reasonably suited to by education, training, or experience — hardest to qualify, cheapest premium. And Social Security is stricter still — no substantial gainful activity, expected to last at least twelve months or end in death. The tell: cannot do your job equals own-occ; cannot do any job equals any-occ; Social Security is always the hardest bar. Two traps to bank — DI policies have no deductibles or coinsurance, they use elimination and benefit periods instead, and benefits pay in arrears, so your first check on a sixty-day elimination period arrives around day ninety, not day sixty."
