        "say": "Alright, this is Regulation and Suitability — the last tab, and the one that ties every product you have studied back to who is in charge and what duty you owe the client. Start with the big structural fact the exam wants: insurance is primarily regulated at the state level, not the federal level. The law that locks that in is the McCarran-Ferguson Act of nineteen forty-five, which limited federal authority over insurance to a narrow slice — antitrust and fair labor — and left the major burden with the states. So each state runs its own show through its own insurance commissioner and department. Now, where does the NAIC fit? The National Association of Insurance Commissioners is all fifty state commissioners together, and it develops model laws — but here is the trap they test: it has no enforcement power. It cannot force any state to adopt anything. What it does have is an accreditation program that nudges states toward consistency, which is how state-level regulation stays coherent without a federal hand on the wheel. Finally, know that federal law still reaches into employee benefits: ERISA governs benefit plans, COBRA handles continuation coverage, F M L A covers family leave, HIPAA is portability and privacy, the A C A sets essential benefits, and Dodd-Frank drives financial reform under S E C and FINRA oversight. So the theme is a state-run system with a few federal benefit laws layered on top."
